**Core Definition:** Encapsulation is the process of binding data members (variables) and member functions (methods) into a single unit, known as a **Class**.
**Data Hiding:** It restricts direct access to an object's internal components. This prevents external code from accidentally modifying or tampering with the data.
**Implementation:** In Python, this is achieved by using **access modifiers** (like prefixing a variable name with a single underscore `_` or double underscore `__` to make it private).

• Global Access by Default: Python provides access to all variables (data) and methods globally by default. If we declare any variable or method outside a class, it is called a global variable or global method. This means we can access it within any class or from anywhere in the program.
• Restricting Access with Encapsulation: By using encapsulation, we can restrict variables and methods from being accessed globally by making them Private or Protected.
