2. Private and Protected (Simple Explanations & How to Access Them)

🔒 Private Members (Double Underscore __) Means Variable only access by that class Function. it also does not access by that class Object outside the class.

• What it means: If we declare a variable or method with a double underscore prefix (like __age), it becomes Private. This means it can only be accessed inside that specific class. Where variable is private and Method is public because using that public method we can access the variable of that class only. You cannot access it directly from outside the class using an object.

• How to access it:
	1. Using Getter/Setter Methods: We create a public function inside the class that returns or changes the private value.
	2. Using Name Mangling: Python internally renames private variables. You can bypass the restriction from outside the class using the format: _ClassName__variableName.
Simple Code Example:
python
class Student:
    def __init__(self):
        self.__name = "Ayan" # Private variable

    # Public method (Getter) to access the private variable safely
    def get_name(self):
        return self.__name

obj = Student()
# print(obj.__name)       # ❌ ERROR! Cannot access directly.
print(obj.get_name())     #  Correct Way 1: Prints "Ayan"
print(obj._Student__name) #  Correct Way 2 (Name Mangling): Prints "Ayan"
Use code with caution.

🛡️ Protected Members (Single Underscore _) Means Variable only accessed by the Class Function and class object.

• What it means: If we declare a variable or method with a single underscore prefix (like _age), it becomes Protected. Technically, this is just a warning/hint to other programmers that this variable should only be accessed inside the same class or its child (sub) classes.
• How to access it: Python does not actually enforce restrictions on protected members. You can still access them directly outside the class using an object just like a regular variable, but doing so violates standard coding practices.
Simple Code Example:
python
class Parent:
    def __init__(self):
        self._address = "123 Pune" # Protected variable

class Child(Parent):
    def display(self):
        print(self._address) # Allowed: Accessing in a child class

obj = Child()
obj.display()      # Prints "123 Pune"
print(obj._address) #  Works, but not recommended by convention!
Use code with caution.
